WEBVTT

00:00:03.250 --> 00:00:04.310
Bombs away.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:08.710
Ten seconds to impact.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:11.860
Eight, seven, six,

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.580
five, four, three,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.620
two, one.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:18.670
Impact.

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.380
Starting BDA.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.230
The target's surface is crumbling.

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:36.270
What is that?

00:01:22.730 --> 00:01:23.270
The incomplete is to blame for all these unknowns

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:26.110
in this moment

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:26.570
The incomplete is to blame for all these unknowns

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.690
They were all hack jobs, so let's perfect them

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:32.390
We can't keep running away

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.200
We can't be prone to it

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.090
There is one thing in our power

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.040
We'll prove it one way or another

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:39.910
We have to go

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:41.710
We have to go

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:43.540
We have to make our move

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.420
We have to make our move

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:49.050
Right now we've got cold feet

00:01:49.050 --> 00:01:51.800
But we'll fight for right

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:52.840
Not everything unanswered needs an answer

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:56.510
Not everything unanswered needs an answer

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:59.020
We decide what our future means, so stand up, honey

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:03.900
The more difficult it is, the more fun it gets

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.400
We decide what our future means, so stand up, honey

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:07.570
We have to be logical, I know

00:02:07.570 --> 00:02:10.320
We have to be logical, I know

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.450
Yeah, I know that much

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:14.030
Let's oppose this crisis

00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:18.620
Let's oppose this crisis

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:21.580
Let's oppose this crisis

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:22.330
Let's oppose this crisis

00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:41.720
Crow 22, this is CP.
The target is heading your way.

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:43.180
Awaiting orders.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:49.610
CP, this is Crow 22. There's too much
red dust. Switch to GPS artillery.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:50.570
Roger that.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:56.110
Target vector 0-9-0, alpha 18645.

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:59.590
Coordinates 0-9-0, alpha 18645.
Roger that.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:02.870
This is CP. Glory 01 dropped two shells.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:56.850
Impact.

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.800
There's one more!

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.240
BDA, report.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.870
There's too much smoke. Hold on.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:55.000
Crow 22, report BDA.
Do we need to strike again?

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:57.240
I repeat. BDA, report.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:04:59.990
Reactive armor?

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:06.260
Rapidly generated tissue appeared to burst
on impact to buffer the shock.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:09.750
Its tissue changed before it was hit.

00:05:10.030 --> 00:05:12.000
The Anguirus in Nigashio

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:17.160
also made a defensive move
before we launched our attack.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:17.960
Yes.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:21.550
In the end, we were able to
take it out with a civilian's help.

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:26.510
The civilian killed it
with that robot, right?

00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:46.490
Backup will be here in a few minutes!

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:49.290
We might be in their belly by then.

00:06:02.920 --> 00:06:04.970
Stand back and don't move!

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:06.760
Don't move?

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:10.930
Move forward!

00:06:11.440 --> 00:06:12.410
Which is it?

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.650
We'll take cover over there!
Keep them away from us.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:19.240
Hey, let's go!

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:19.240
Roger that.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.450
Yes! Crush 'em!

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:31.290
Go! Beat 'em all up!

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:32.500
Keep your voice down!

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.870
Jet Jaguar, what's our battery level?

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.920
Twenty-three percent.

00:06:37.420 --> 00:06:39.840
It won't last against so many of them.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:46.470
There <i>is</i> a supercomputer!

00:06:46.470 --> 00:06:48.720
It bends time, whirls around,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.600
and there are countless eyes that look this way!

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:54.230
We need a supercomputer to make Archetype.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:57.480
And with Archetype, we can make a supercomputer.

00:06:57.480 --> 00:06:58.480
But then that means...

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:02.740
We need Archetype to make Archetype.

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:04.780
Which comes first? The chicken or the egg?

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:07.620
The answer is a computer
that uses the Singular Point?

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.620
The Orthogonal Diagonalizer can be
completed but can't be used

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:13.460
because of the Catastrophe.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.500
So I'm researching the Catastrophe.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:16.620
The Catastrophe?

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:19.380
Catastrophe? Did she dump you already?

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:20.500
What's this all about?

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:23.760
The fate of the world may be in her hands.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:26.340
She's what now?

00:07:24.700 --> 00:07:26.340
There here is! There's Kanda!

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.220
Dead bodies too.

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:29.640
Were they eaten?

00:07:29.640 --> 00:07:31.260
It's the office staff.

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.310
He's moving! He's alive!

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:37.600
We arrived at the marina just now.

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:41.820
Yes. Okay. Then we'll be on our way.

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:43.170
Are they alive?

00:07:43.170 --> 00:07:45.110
These are the spider monsters?

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:52.370
Run!

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:56.070
Hello? Hello!?

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:56.070
I'll call you back!

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:00.420
What do we do? Can we go help them at all?

00:08:00.420 --> 00:08:02.090
Jet Jaguar, can you go?

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.630
I need time.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:07.760
Let's do what we can now and
save that guy all tangled up in the web.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:09.050
Pardon me...

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:12.540
That over there is our ship.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:13.930
A ship...

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:15.890
But the key is in the office...

00:08:17.100 --> 00:08:18.730
Wait—Kanda has one!

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:24.320
Let's go, Yun! Take command of Jet Jaguar to
tackle any adversity and save the world!

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:28.530
I can't believe this is happening.

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:36.080
"The Catastrophe is an error created
by the clash of Singular Points."

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:38.290
Then isn't the problem the future itself

00:08:38.290 --> 00:08:40.880
and not the computer?

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:41.880
What?

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:43.710
Maybe they're growing.

00:08:43.710 --> 00:08:46.130
Growing? The Singular Points?

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:49.430
The Super Dimension Calculator might be just showing the future.

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:53.970
Maybe the Singular Points'
uncontrollable growth is the Catastrophe.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:56.680
If the future shows a catastrophe...

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:59.640
There will be a catastrophe
in the world one day.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:03.290
What does that make the Catastrophe, then?
What's going to happen?

00:09:04.570 --> 00:09:06.650
I think "when" is a better question to ask...

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:09.070
There were notes about dates.

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:14.450
The Super Dimension Calculator may have
been used to draw a map of the future.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:19.080
What if this is a bifurcation diagram
of the Singular Points' evolution?

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:21.290
This is the Ashihara Catastrophe...

00:09:21.290 --> 00:09:24.590
He must've thought
the universe would face a catastrophe.

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:27.130
Year 2030.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:29.970
Is that when he thinks it'll happen?

00:09:29.970 --> 00:09:31.630
Isn't that really close!?

00:09:31.630 --> 00:09:32.720
Look at this part.

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:40.980
It might indicate the end of the world
due to the Catastrophe.

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:47.230
Was he trying to prevent the Catastrophe
with the Super Dimension Calculator?

00:09:47.780 --> 00:09:49.190
I have to let everyone know.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:52.870
Professor Li!

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:56.410
I've seen her before.

00:09:56.410 --> 00:09:58.290
This is Mei Kamino.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:02.920
This is Tilda.
You've met Steven, and this is BB.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:06.170
Kamino? You wrote that report.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:10.300
Will you explain what you found out?

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:11.470
All right.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:17.430
Professor Ashihara developed the Super Dimension Calculator for his research.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:23.390
His theory was that Archetype is an
otherworldly molecule created from Singular Points.

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:26.300
He was right. It's been proven.

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:28.000
You call that proof?

00:10:29.080 --> 00:10:31.780
He saw a catastrophe in the future.

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:33.070
The future?

00:10:33.070 --> 00:10:35.870
A catastrophe in the future?

00:10:35.870 --> 00:10:40.210
Yes. The catastrophe isn't something
that happens to the computer.

00:10:40.210 --> 00:10:42.200
It's going to happen to the world.

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:43.600
I don't believe it.

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.370
Look, think about it!

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:49.130
It's a computer that can actually see the future!

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:51.050
Maybe it's a malfunction.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:52.950
A malfunction of the universe.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:55.200
A catastrophe to the world?

00:10:55.200 --> 00:10:56.590
That's why we have to stop it.

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:59.100
Do you have any evidence?

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:00.470
Yes. Pero 2!

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:01.610
Right here!

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:07.690
According to Super Dimension
Calculator D23-283846 in Ashihara's notes...

00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.630
"If the radiance of a thousand suns

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.840
were to burst at once into the sky,

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:36.510
that would be like the splendor
of the Mighty One."

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:40.140
"I am become Death,
the Shatterer of Worlds."

00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:42.690
A quote from the Bhagavad Gita?

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:44.090
The evidence is a scripture?

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:47.650
No—if the future catastrophe is real,

00:11:47.650 --> 00:11:52.860
the computer will function safely
if it doesn't come in contact with it.

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:58.700
Make it loop repeatedly in the near future
before the Catastrophe.

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:00.160
What do you mean?

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.290
The Super Dimension Calculator
is already usable.

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:04.870
What about combinatorial explosion?

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:06.580
Circumventable, in theory.

00:12:06.580 --> 00:12:08.790
Can you complete it?

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:12.590
We have to do something
about the Catastrophe first.

00:12:12.590 --> 00:12:16.510
If we can complete the Orthogonal Diagonalizer,

00:12:16.510 --> 00:12:18.720
it will protect Earth from red dust.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.640
You agree, don't you?

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.550
The Catastrophe may occur any minute...

00:12:22.550 --> 00:12:26.020
If we can use the computer,
it'll change everything.

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:29.310
We can eliminate red dust from the world.

00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:35.480
Worth trying, don't you think?
We have the knowledge and tools.

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:37.320
Li, what do you think?

00:12:37.320 --> 00:12:40.820
Right. BB, get ready to activate the computer.

00:12:40.820 --> 00:12:42.830
What are you saying, Professor Li!?

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:46.130
Get back to Upala right away. I'll arrange a flight.

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:48.790
Not from Heathrow but somewhere else.

00:12:48.790 --> 00:12:52.730
The completion of the Orthogonal Diagonalizer
is the top priority.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:55.340
The prevention of the Catastrophe is!

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:57.840
Salunga disappeared?

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:01.180
The red dust has disappeared,
and there's no sensor reaction either.

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:03.590
It went somewhere with red dust?

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:07.310
A flock of Rodan is approaching.
We have to leave now.

00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:10.230
We're off to London. Let's meet in India.

00:13:10.230 --> 00:13:12.810
Right. I'll be in touch.

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:19.740
You have to trust me. Get ready to go.

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:21.860
Okay.

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:27.160
The flock of Rodan has crossed
the defense line and the coastal barrier

00:13:27.160 --> 00:13:29.120
and is flying towards London.

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:31.460
They're moving extremely fast.

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:33.420
People are evacuating...

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:35.960
Gotta hurry, gotta hurry!

00:13:36.310 --> 00:13:38.960
Eek, we gotta hurry!

00:13:41.850 --> 00:13:42.720
What's this?

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:46.720
I also think the Catastrophe is real.

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:47.850
Professor Li.

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:52.230
This was Ashihara's mansion.
It's under my care now.

00:13:52.230 --> 00:13:54.480
I couldn't decipher his notes, however.

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:58.280
But your explanation made many things clear.

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:01.590
Singular Points are getting
more active across the world right now.

00:14:01.590 --> 00:14:06.780
It could be the trigger for the Catastrophe,
and that monster in Tokyo could be the start of it.

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:09.290
Godzilla is a Singular Point?

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:13.750
So how do we avoid a catastrophe
that's already part of the future?

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:16.090
How to change the future...

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:18.250
You're getting close to the answer.

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:21.470
You have to look where Ashihara didn't.

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:24.550
You have to go further than him.

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:30.410
We've got a flight. Let's go.

00:14:33.890 --> 00:14:34.790
Pero 2!

00:14:34.790 --> 00:14:35.650
Coming!

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:37.150
Please hurry.

00:14:41.240 --> 00:14:42.150
Get on!

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:49.280
The airfield is an hour from here.
I hope we make it in time.

00:14:54.250 --> 00:14:55.500
Stop wailin'.

00:14:55.500 --> 00:14:57.380
We'll get you out.

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:01.300
It's faster this way.

00:15:02.010 --> 00:15:03.340
Where's the key?

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:05.050
What key!?

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:06.760
Oh, in my chest pocket...!

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:08.590
I sincerely believe in Amitabha, Lord have mercy...

00:15:12.390 --> 00:15:13.560
It's right behind them.

00:15:14.940 --> 00:15:15.940
It's coming!

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:19.150
Jet Jaguar, get out of here. Let's go.

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:20.150
Roger that.

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:54.140
Right, clear. Left, clear. Let's go.

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:57.390
We're heading to a ship roughly 100m to the west.

00:15:57.390 --> 00:15:58.020
All right.

00:15:58.020 --> 00:16:00.980
Run and don't stop! Don't look back!

00:16:01.730 --> 00:16:04.480
Behind us! No! In the air!

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:16.710
Jet Jaguar, hurry!

00:16:16.710 --> 00:16:18.920
I'll catch up with you.

00:16:29.660 --> 00:16:30.690
Jet Jaguar!

00:16:40.690 --> 00:16:43.860
The migration of Rodan across the Atlantic...

00:16:46.280 --> 00:16:48.630
Welcome back. You're home early.

00:16:49.780 --> 00:16:53.160
I'm going away for a while.
Go stay with your aunt.

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:54.830
I don't like her food.

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:58.600
Suck it up. Or would you rather
come to the lab with me?

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:00.920
An unexpected appearance...

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:02.960
Rewind that.

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:04.800
I can't. It's live.

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:08.470
Is this a different case? Then...

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:12.760
You have to help.

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:15.060
I'm proceeding as per instructions,

00:17:15.060 --> 00:17:17.060
but the problem in Tokyo seems to be solved.

00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:19.560
They don't need it now, do they?

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:22.690
You don't actually believe it's solved, do you?

00:17:22.690 --> 00:17:25.110
She might be able to stop the Catastrophe.

00:17:25.110 --> 00:17:26.940
You have a lot of faith in her.

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:30.950
Do what you have to, but don't get in her way.

00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:33.660
Okay. We'll move along as planned.

00:17:33.660 --> 00:17:35.240
But...

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:37.490
The signal is very weak.

00:17:37.490 --> 00:17:39.290
Let me know when it gets better.

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:40.360
You got it.

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:43.750
I'll reintroduce you to BB in India.

00:17:44.420 --> 00:17:48.260
He'll help us monitor the Catastrophe
with the computer.

00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:51.630
The concept is too difficult for Steven and Tilda.

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:54.890
But the Catastrophe could happen any second...

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:59.070
Yes, but we first have to pinpoint when.

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:01.520
Is that even possible?

00:18:02.390 --> 00:18:07.650
If we were able to prevent it,
there'd be nothing to see in the future.

00:18:08.110 --> 00:18:11.360
Something that hasn't happened
never existed to begin with.

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:13.160
We're at a dead end.

00:18:13.550 --> 00:18:17.810
If there's no catastrophe in the future,
isn't that the ultimate solution?

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:19.320
Since it never existed.

00:18:19.320 --> 00:18:20.330
But...

00:18:20.330 --> 00:18:22.540
It's a higher-dimensional phenomenon.

00:18:23.330 --> 00:18:25.420
We'll have to see what comes next.

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:29.800
Tilda and Steven won't like it,
but I'm more concerned about BB.

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:33.180
He'll probably want to use
the Catastrophe for something else.

00:18:33.800 --> 00:18:35.920
Use the Catastrophe?

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:40.970
Rodan.

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:56.950
We better leave the car here.

00:18:56.950 --> 00:18:58.300
Let's go to that building.

00:18:59.120 --> 00:19:00.040
Let's go.

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:02.580
We have to hurry.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:05.580
I forgot my notes.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:07.710
Staying alive is more important.

00:19:13.050 --> 00:19:14.400
Molly!

00:19:18.670 --> 00:19:20.080
Keep going.

00:19:20.080 --> 00:19:21.180
Professor Li!

00:19:35.860 --> 00:19:38.740
Professor Li! Run!

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.500
Buggin' off? Come and fight!

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.750
That's the last thing we want!

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:51.750
Hurry, to the ship!

00:19:51.750 --> 00:19:53.840
Are they returning to protect their nest?

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:55.090
Current location?

00:19:55.090 --> 00:19:56.630
Entering field of vision.

00:19:59.970 --> 00:20:04.650
Battery, running out.
Switching to emergency protocol.

00:20:04.650 --> 00:20:07.350
Purging battery to attempt escape.

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:09.680
Go ahead to the ship.

00:20:17.900 --> 00:20:19.280
Jet Jaguar?

00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:24.080
Protocol, set. Purging backpack.

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:28.830
Hup! Hup! Hup!

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:50.790
Yes!

00:20:50.790 --> 00:20:51.610
Start the ship.

00:20:51.610 --> 00:20:52.400
Got it!

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:14.170
Backup battery, low.
Entering sleep mode. Good night.

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:37.560
Ashihara?

00:21:43.160 --> 00:21:45.120
Yun, come here.

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:03.840
Has the Catastrophe begun?

00:22:06.560 --> 00:22:12.310
I woke up to your voice that painted my vision with color

00:22:12.310 --> 00:22:18.190
And it was the same shade of curiosity I find in my familiar daily life

00:22:18.190 --> 00:22:23.570
If this feeling can grow naturally on its own

00:22:23.570 --> 00:22:29.450
Will it fall off one day like an unripe fruit, I wonder?

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:34.080
I don't know anything

00:22:34.080 --> 00:22:38.460
Not even anything about you

00:22:41.340 --> 00:22:46.970
Let me keep on dreaming for now

00:22:46.970 --> 00:22:51.350
If this moment is all I have now

00:22:51.810 --> 00:22:55.810
I can still feel the warmth in my palm

00:22:57.650 --> 00:23:03.070
My thrumming feelings open and burst out

00:23:03.070 --> 00:23:07.080
I want to keep learning more, like I am now

00:23:08.830 --> 00:23:13.620
This pulsating rhythm I feel just won't stop

00:23:13.620 --> 00:23:14.580
It won't stop
